\label{Back and forth system definition}
Suppose ${\ensuremath{\mathcal{M}}}$ and ${\ensuremath{\mathcal{N}}}$ are $\ensuremath{\mathfrak{L}}_c$-structures which have an $\ensuremath{\mathfrak{L}}_a$-geometry. For $\alpha \in \operatorname{ORD}$, a {\bf{back and forth system}} (of length $\alpha$) between ${\ensuremath{\mathcal{M}}}$ and ${\ensuremath{\mathcal{N}}}$ is a sequence $\<I_i\>_{i \leq \alpha}$ which satisfies the following.  
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] For $i < j \leq \alpha$ we have $I_j \subseteq I_i$ and $I_\alpha \neq \emptyset$.

\item[(b)] For each $i \leq \alpha$, the set $I_i$ consists of pairs of closed finite tuples $({\ensuremath{\mathbf{a}}}, {\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}})$ of the same length, where ${\ensuremath{\mathbf{a}}} \subseteq {\ensuremath{\mathcal{M}}}$ and ${\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}} \subseteq {\ensuremath{\mathcal{N}}}$.

\item[(c)] If $\ell \leq \alpha$ and $(\<a_i\>_{i \in [n]}, \<b_i\>_{i \in [n]}) \in I_\ell$, then for all relation symbols $R \in \ensuremath{\mathfrak{L}}_c^k$ and all sequences $\<j_i\>_{i \in [k]} \subseteq [n]$, we have
\[
{\ensuremath{\mathcal{M}}} \models R(a_{j_0}, \dots, a_{j_{k-1}})\text{ if and only if }{\ensuremath{\mathcal{N}}} \models R(b_{j_0}, \dots, b_{j_{k-1}}).
\]

\item[(d)] For each $i < j \leq \alpha$ and each pair $({\ensuremath{\mathbf{a}}}, {\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}}) \in I_{j}$, 

\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] if ${\ensuremath{\mathbf{a}}}^* \subseteq {\ensuremath{\mathcal{M}}}$ and ${\ensuremath{\mathbf{a}}}{\ensuremath{\mathbf{a}}}^*$ is closed then there is a ${\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}}^* \subseteq {\ensuremath{\mathcal{N}}}$ such that $({\ensuremath{\mathbf{a}}}{\ensuremath{\mathbf{a}}}^*, {\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}}{\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}}^*) \in I_i$, and

\item[(ii)] if ${\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}}^* \subseteq {\ensuremath{\mathcal{N}}}$ and ${\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}}{\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}}^*$ is closed then there is a ${\ensuremath{\mathbf{a}}}^* \subseteq {\ensuremath{\mathcal{M}}}$ such that $({\ensuremath{\mathbf{a}}}{\ensuremath{\mathbf{a}}}^*, {\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}}{\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}}^*) \in I_i$.
\end{itemize}

\end{itemize}

We say that ${\ensuremath{\mathcal{M}}}$ and ${\ensuremath{\mathcal{N}}}$ are {\bf{$\alpha$-equivalent}}, denoted by ${\ensuremath{\mathcal{M}}} \equiv_\alpha {\ensuremath{\mathcal{N}}}$, if there is a back and forth system of length $\alpha$, each component of which is non-empty.

Suppose $\mathcal{I}$ is an invariant diagram and $\ensuremath{\mathop{\pmb{\tau}}}$ is an invariant system compatible with $\mathcal{I}$. A {\bf{weak Knight system}} (for $\mathcal{I}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathop{\pmb{\tau}}}$) is a sequence $\<K_\beta\>_{\beta \in \ensuremath{\omega}_1}$ satisfying the following for each $\beta \in \ensuremath{\omega}_1$.  
\begin{itemize}
\item Each $K_\beta$ is an isomorphism closed collection of countable $(\ensuremath{\mathfrak{L}}_b \cup \ensuremath{\mathfrak{L}}_c)$-structures compatible with $\mathcal{I}$ and with rank at most $\beta$.

\item For all $\alpha < \beta$ and all ${\ensuremath{\mathcal{M}}} \in K_\beta$, ${\ensuremath{\mathcal{M}}} \models 
